WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.270
Hey everyone!

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:08.020
No math here, I just wanted to post two quick
announcements for you.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:12.190
First, the number of you who have opted to
subscribe to this channel has once again rolled

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:15.700
over a power of 2, which is just mind-boggling
to me.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.910
I’m still touched that there’s so many
of you who enjoy math like this, just for

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:25.350
fun, and who have helped support the channel
by watching, by sharing the content here,

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:29.800
and for many of you on Patreon, very directly
supporting it.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.190
Really, each one of the 2^19 of you means
a lot to me.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.840
As a thanks, and just for fun, I’ll be doing
a second Q&amp;A session.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.700
I’ve left a link to where you can ask and
upvote questions on the screen here and in

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.700
the description, and just like with the first
one I’ll answer the questions in the podcast

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.470
I do with Ben Eater and Ben Stenhuag, since
I think discussions are more fun than answers

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:52.470
in a vacuum.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:56.800
By the way, if you guys don’t already know
about Ben Eater’s channel, what are ya’

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.800
doing!

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.050
This man is the Bob Ross of computer engineering.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:03.989
If you want to understand how a computer works,
and I mean really understand it from the ground

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:07.219
up, his channel is the place to go.

00:01:07.219 --> 00:01:09.929
Trust me, he is a very good explainer.

00:01:09.929 --> 00:01:13.750
Anyway, the reason I bring him up is that
he and I just recorded a pretty interesting

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.090
conversation about Net Neutrality.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:20.750
I want to be very clear: Ben Eater is not
against net neutrality, and nor am I.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.829
However, the issue is a lot more nuanced than
I first realized.

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:31.060
And because there are already many great videos
about Net Neutrality, and why it’s a good

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:35.849
thing, to offer content you may not have seen
before this conversation was a chance to unearth

00:01:35.849 --> 00:01:37.829
some of the tradeoffs at play here.

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:43.360
You see, the thing about Eater is that before
he was creating educational content, he worked

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.609
for many years in the networking industry,
so he has a pretty clear view of both sides

00:01:47.609 --> 00:01:48.789
of this equation.

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:55.209
I’m going to play you just a minute of it
here, and then link to the full video if you

00:01:55.209 --> 00:01:59.469
want to go see it on Ben’s channel, which
you should be going to check out anyway.

00:01:59.469 --> 00:02:04.749
For those of you who would prefer to listen
to a 45-minute conversation in podcast form,

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:45.570
we did also publish it as an episode 

00:02:45.570 --> 00:03:45.100
of Ben, Ben, and Blue.

